[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] qualified for the [[UEFA Cup]] by finishing fourth in the [[1999–2000 FA Premier League]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/eng00.html |title=England 1999/2000 |publisher=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |date=15 April 2001 |access-date=9 April 2020 |first1=Ken |last1=Butler |first2=Stephen |last2=Mulrine |first3=Andreas |last3=Exenberger }}</ref> Their opposition in the first round was [[FC Rapid București|Rapid București]] of Romania. The first leg was held at Rapid's home ground [[Stadionul Giulești-Valentin Stănescu (1939)|Stadionul Giuleşti-Valentin Stănescu]], where [[Nick Barmby]] gave Liverpool a 1–0 win with a goal in the 28th minute.<ref>{{cite news |title=Reds make Rapid progress |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/923502.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=14 September 2000 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> The second leg at Liverpool's home ground, [[Anfield]], finished 0–0, which meant that Liverpool won the tie 1–0 on [[Playoff format#Total points series (aggregate)|aggregate]] to progress to the second round.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/945263.stm |title=Reds achieve Rapid progress |website=BBC Sport |date=28 September 2000 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> Liverpool faced [[FC Slovan Liberec|Slovan Liberec]] of the Czech Republic in the second round. The first leg at Anfield was heading for a 0–0 draw, until the 87th minute when [[Emile Heskey]] scored to give Liverpool a 1–0 victory.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/990856.stm |title=Heskey rescues poor Liverpool |website=BBC Sport |date=26 October 2000 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> The second leg was at Liberec's home ground, the [[Stadion u Nisy]]. Liberec took the lead in the first half to level the tie at 1–1. Midway through the first half, Liverpool equalised to make it 1–1 on the night and 2–1 in their favour on aggregate. Two further goals in the second half by Barmby and [[Michael Owen]], before a late Liberec goal, ensured Liverpool won the match 3–2 to progress to the third round after a 4–2 aggregate victory.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1013792.stm |title=Owen goal seals Reds comeback |website=BBC Sport |date=9 November 2000 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref>

[[Olympiacos F.C.|Olympiacos]] of Greece were their opponents in the third round. The first leg was held at Olympiacos' home ground, the [[Karaiskakis Stadium]]. Liverpool were heading for a 2–1 victory courtesy of goals from Barmby and [[Steven Gerrard]], until Olympiacos equalised in the last minute to earn a 2–2 draw.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1037752.stm |title=FT: Olympiakos 2–2 Liverpool |website=BBC Sport |date=23 November 2000 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> The second leg at Anfield was won 2–0 by Liverpool, with a goal scored in each half by Barmby and Heskey respectively. The victory ensured Liverpool won the tie 4–2 on aggregate to progress to the fourth round.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1059115.stm |title=Liverpool enjoy Greek cruise |website=BBC Sport |date=7 December 2000 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref>

Liverpool faced the Italian side [[AS Roma|Roma]] in the fourth round. The first leg was held at Roma's home ground the [[Stadio Olimpico]], where Liverpool had won the [[European Cup]] twice in [[1977 European Cup Final|1977]] and [[1984 European Cup Final|1984]]. Incidentally, Roma were the team Liverpool beat to win the European Cup in 1984.<ref>{{cite news |title=Capello ready for tough test |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1166663.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=12 February 2001 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> Liverpool were once again successful at the Stadio Olimpico, as they won 2–0 courtesy of two Owen goals in the second-half.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1172591.stm |title=Liverpool stun Roma with Owen brace |website=BBC Sport |date=15 February 2001 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> The second leg at Anfield was a close affair. Roma scored in the 70th minute to take the lead, and needed to score another goal to take the match into [[Overtime (sports)|extra-time]]. They looked like they had the opportunity to do so when the referee awarded a penalty towards the end of the match after he had adjudged that [[Markus Babbel]] had handled the ball. Moments later, he reversed his decision and instead awarded Roma a corner-kick. Roma were unable to score the necessary goal following the incident and Liverpool progressed to the quarter-finals courtesy of a 2–1 aggregate victory.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool survive Roma onslaught |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1183479.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=22 February 2011 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref>

Portuguese side [[FC Porto|Porto]] were the opposition in the quarter-finals. The first leg in Portugal ended 0–0.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/uefa_cup/1208920.stm |title=Liverpool force stalemate |website=BBC Sport |date=8 March 2001 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> Liverpool won the second leg at Anfield 2–0. [[Danny Murphy (footballer born 1977)|Danny Murphy]] and Owen scored in the first half to progress to the semi-finals courtesy of a 2–0 aggregate victory.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/1223448.stm |title=Clockwatch: Liverpool 2–0 Porto |website=BBC Sport |date=15 March 2001 |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> Liverpool were drawn against Spanish side [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in the semi-finals. Liverpool defended resolutely during the first leg at Barcelona's ground the [[Camp Nou]] to earn a 0–0 draw.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/apr/06/uefa.sport |title=Liverpool keep it level-headed |work=The Guardian|date=6 April 2001 |location=London |first=Dominic |last=Fifield |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> Houllier defended his tactics after the match, stating: "If I'd gone out and attacked and lost by three goals, you would be calling me naive. What's the point in being naive? That would be a betrayal to our supporters."<ref>{{harvtxt|Wilson|Murray|2013|p=322}}</ref> The second leg at Anfield was equally close, until the 44th minute when Liverpool were awarded a penalty. [[Gary McAllister]] scored the subsequent penalty to put Liverpool 1–0 up in the match and the tie; a Barcelona goal would see them progress as a result of the [[away goals rule]]. Liverpool managed to see out the 90 minutes without conceding a goal to progress to their first European final since they were banned from participating in Europe following the [[Heysel Stadium disaster]] at the [[1985 European Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/apr/19/minutebyminute.sport |title=Liverpool 1 v 0 Barcelona |work=The Guardian|location=London |date=19 April 2001 |first=Matt |last=Hughes |access-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref>

